module tasks.`2026-11-19-ui-kit-four-platforms`

task UiKitFourPlatforms {
  title "M0a: the UI kit builds and draws text on macOS, Linux, iOS and Android"
  verify $ make check-ui-targets && cargo nextest run -p bboxy450-render -p bboxy450-ui -p bboxy450-ui-core
  paths "crates/bbt-render", "crates/bbt-ui", "crates/bbt-ui-model", "Makefile", "rust-toolchain.toml", "Cargo.toml", "Cargo.lock", "verification/coverage.toml", "ATTRIBUTION.md", "scripts/check-ui-targets.sh"
  brief """
    Our stand (verbatim, `specs/philosophy.md`): Find every abstraction, dependency, framework, and layer that exists only to compensate for the complexity introduced by another layer. Question every inherited architectural decision. Don't make my software more like Linux. Make it more like DOS. Everything should be inspectable, understandable, and modifiable. Subtract before you add. Minimize concepts, not lines of code. Report what you removed.

    ## Goal
    `bboxy450-ui-core`, `bboxy450-ui` and `bboxy450-render` compile for `aarch64-apple-darwin`, `aarch64-apple-ios`, `aarch64-apple-ios-sim`, `aarch64-linux-android` and `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, and they draw real text on every one of them (round 4 §1). The mobile app (`bboxy450-term-mobile`) and a future Linux desktop build on this.

    ## Steps
    1. **Apple gate.** In `crates/bbt-render` (`text/mod.rs`, `text/terminal.rs`, `chrome/text.rs`, `chrome/text_glyphs.rs`, `Cargo.toml`), change every `target_os = "macos"` that selects CoreText to `target_vendor = "apple"`, so iOS uses CoreText. Fix whatever does not exist on iOS: for example, a macOS-only CoreGraphics call or a hard-coded font such as Menlo. Use the iOS system equivalents, and name each substitution in the report.
    2. **Portable text backend.** Add one module, `text/portable.rs`, that uses `swash` for shaping, scaling and colour glyphs where possible, with a bundled font. JetBrains Mono is already in `crates/bbt-render/tests/fonts/`; move it, or another OFL UI font, into the crate and record it in `ATTRIBUTION.md` and the license checks (`make test-licenses`). It provides the same API as the macOS module: `Font`, `Fonts`, `measure`, `x_height`, `rasterize`, `rasterize_at`.
       - Compile the module on **every** target and select it with cfg only on non-Apple targets. Its tests then run on this Mac host.
       - Delete the `Err("... requires CoreText/macOS")` stubs it replaces.
    3. **wgpu backends per target.** Use `metal` on Apple targets, and `vulkan` plus `gles` on Android and Linux, through target-specific dependency tables. Leave the macOS feature set byte-identical.
    4. **The gate.** Add `make check-ui-targets`, which runs `rustup target add` for any missing target and then `cargo check` of the three crates for each of the five targets. Install the targets through `rust-toolchain.toml` `targets = [...]`, not by global configuration. The Android check must not need the NDK; if it does, report which crate needs it and why.
    5. **Tests.** Add a portable-backend test that rasterizes "Hello, swarm" and asserts a non-empty mask and plausible metrics. Add one chrome-text measure test that runs through the portable backend on the host.
    6. Make sure nothing else changes on macOS. Run the crates' existing tests, and run `make test-parity` once on the final commit, because this touches render code.

    ## Acceptance
    - [ ] `make check-ui-targets` exits 0 for all five targets.
    - [ ] The portable backend's tests pass on the host, and the CoreText stubs are deleted.
    - [ ] `make test-parity` is unchanged on macOS.
    - [ ] The report lists what was removed, and every iOS substitution.

    ## Rules
    - Read first: [[specs/mobile]], [[d1794292861627536-remote-nodes-round-4-mobile-both-platforms]], [[epics/autonomous-nodes-and-mobile]], and the repository `AGENTS.md` and `LAND.md`.
    - Work only in your worktree and the allowed paths. Use signed commits as Boxy <bboxy450@gmail.com>. Run one build or test at a time, and only targeted tests while you iterate (CPU is shared).
    - Land through `make land` yourself when verification is green (autonomous landing is authorized). Then write the report at `reports/2026-11-19-<slug>.den` and send `task_report` with the commit SHA and each command with its real exit status.
    - Unattended only: no operator clicks or prompts, and no desktop capture.
    - Latitude: you choose internal structure, names and test layout. Stop and message the Oracle only if an invariant or spec line would have to break.


